<p>The First Street Federal Building was listed in the National Register of Historic Places in 1985 as a contributing structure in the Mandan Commercial Historic District. Built in 1915 as a U.S. post office, it served that purpose until a new post office opened in 1962. The building was then transferred to the Mandan Library District and housed the library from 1964 through 2003.</p>

<p>{% person "james-knox-taylor" %}James Knox Taylor{% endperson %}, supervising architect of the U.S. Treasury Department from 1897 through 1912, designed the building concurrently with a similar but slightly smaller federal building in Wahpeton. Taylor became known as the "national architect" because of improvements in federal design and construction during his 15-year tenure. By the end of his federal career, the Treasury commissioned an average of ten buildings per month nationwide. Taylor participated in the construction of more than 800 federal buildings, helping create a recognizable federal architectural identity. His successor, {% person "oscar-wenderoth" %}Oscar Wenderoth{% endperson %}, supervised the Mandan building's construction and commissioning.</p>

<p>Taylor sought to harmonize federal architecture with local history, climate, and environment, specifying North Dakota brick for the building. Although smaller and less ornate than many period federal buildings, its main elevation was carefully proportioned to create a dignified, solid character. Iron lamps, recessed windows, and elegant arches contributed to an effect based on unity, scale, symmetry, rhythm, and classical ornament.</p>

