<p>The National Geodetic Survey selected the "massive structure" to hold an elevation benchmark in 1931. The marker was installed near the building's southwestern corner, on the southern wall approximately five feet above ground. A 1982 survey team could not locate it, reportedly because a bush concealed both the marker and cornerstone. Another possible explanation is that the team searched the current post office two blocks away. The database was updated in 2007 to report the marker in good condition.</p>

<p>The marker's outer ring reads "U.S. Coast &amp; Geodetic Survey Benchmark." The second ring records an elevation of 1,651.368 feet above mean sea level, and the center warns of a $250 fine or imprisonment for disturbing it.</p>

<p>After the post office moved in 1962, the building was transferred to the city library district. Since 1939, the library had occupied the basement of the World War Memorial Building, now City Hall. Following remodeling, the former post office served as the library until 2004, when the collection moved to the Northern Pacific Freight House on West Main Street.</p>

<p>The city solicited purchase proposals in 2003, but interest was limited because remediation of the downtown petroleum plume had not begun. The vacant building was left unheated, prompting preservation advocates to accuse the city of allowing demolition by neglect. It was razed in January 2008 for additional senior housing.</p>
